Schizophrenia
Mental Health & Substance Use
Delusions, Auditory hallucinations, Disorganized speech

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-045
Reason for Visit: Acute severe distress, paranoia, and refusal of food due to worsening auditory hallucinations and paranoid delusions.
Location: Private residence (Supported accommodation)
Present at Visit: Patient and support worker
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted that the patient had barricaded their bedroom door, was shouting back at auditory hallucinations, and had refused all food and water for 36 hours due to a delusion of poisoning. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to assess hydration status, rule out acute delirium, and provide pharmacological stabilization to prevent the necessity of an involuntary emergency department transfer.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The voices are screaming that the government put tracking dust in the tap water. They are going to melt my brain if I drink it. Make them stop!"
History of Present Illness: The patient has a chronic history of schizophrenia, currently experiencing an acute exacerbation. They report prominent delusions, specifically bizarre and paranoid beliefs regarding government tracking. The patient suffers from continuous auditory hallucinations consisting of running commentary and conversing voices instructing them not to eat or drink. There is disorganized speech, such as frequent derailment and incoherence during the interview. The support worker notes baseline negative symptoms including affective flattening, diminished emotional expression, alogia (poverty of speech), and avolition with a severe lack of initiative, but today these are overshadowed by the acute positive symptoms. The patient has profound social and occupational dysfunction persisting for years, alongside cognitive deficits in executive functioning.
Functional Status: Currently highly paranoid, severely agitated, and refusing basic physiological needs.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of dehydration; high risk of absconding or impulsive behavior driven by command hallucinations.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/75 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Unkempt, intense, suspicious gaze, constantly tilting head as if listening to an unseen person, dry lips.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals mild tachycardia (likely dehydration and anxiety). Oral mucosa is dry. Abdomen is soft, non-tender. Neurological exam reveals no focal deficits, no rigidity, and no fever (ruling out Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome). Mental state examination is profoundly abnormal: marked paranoid delusions, active auditory hallucinations, and disorganized speech are prominently displayed. Insight is entirely absent.
Environmental Observations: All taps in the bedroom and en-suite are taped shut. The patient is huddled in the corner furthest from the window.
Medication Reconciliation: Support worker reports the patient has been "cheeking" (hiding and spitting out) their prescribed oral Risperidone for the past week.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Schizophrenia - Status: Exacerbated (Acute psychotic relapse)
Differentials:

Delirium - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fluctuating consciousness, absence of severe systemic illness (fever, infection), and the hallucinations being auditory rather than typically visual as seen in delirium.

Schizoaffective Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of a concurrent major mood episode (neither severe mania nor profound melancholic depression is present, only severe psychosis).

Methamphetamine-Induced Psychosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the known chronic history, lack of acute sympathomimetic signs, and the controlled environment of the supported accommodation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, severe psychotic relapse characterized by paranoid delusions and command hallucinations leading to a dangerous refusal of hydration. Telephone triage correctly flagged the dehydration risk and severe agitation. Urgent after-hours medical assessment was vital to physically assess the patient for acute dehydration or medication side effects (e.g., NMS), establish rapport to negotiate oral intake, and administer an acute antipsychotic dose. Without an in-person, home-based intervention to de-escalate the paranoia and provide safe hydration, the patient would have required an immediate, highly traumatic involuntary police transfer to the hospital.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Successfully negotiated the patient to drink one sealed, factory-packaged bottle of water to break the dehydration cycle. Administered oral Quetiapine 50mg STAT as a short-acting sedative/antipsychotic rescue dose to downregulate the acute paranoia and induce sleep. Expected investigation results (outpatient): neurocognitive testing revealing deficits in executive function.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement "Sealed Intake Agreement." Due to the fixed delusion about tap water, instruct the support worker to provide only sealed, commercial bottles of water and pre-packaged food for the next 48 hours. Reward the patient's consumption of these items with reduced clinical pressure and validation of their safety strategy. This works around the fixed delusion (which cannot be reasoned away) to achieve the operant goal of maintaining hydration and nutrition.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the community mental health team for assessment of transitioning to a long-acting injectable (LAI) antipsychotic due to oral non-compliance.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: Community mental health team to review tomorrow; GP review in 48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient refuses all fluids (even sealed ones) for another 12 hours, if they attempt to physically harm staff due to paranoia, or if they abscond from the facility.

